The following forum message was posted by kapatp at http://sourceforge.net/projects/rkward/forums/forum/165574/topic/3692612:

@m-eik: As I mentioned in the other thread on \'static windows binary\' thread (http://thread.gmane.org/gmane.comp.statistics.rkward.devel/735) this is an really useful idea.

For clarification, wouldn\'t the settings file (rkwardrc : ~/.kde/share/config/rkwardrc) and the output directory (~/.rkward/) be readonly then?

The following forum message was posted by tfry at http://sourceforge.net/projects/rkward/forums/forum/165574/topic/3692612:

@m-eik: Good idea to simply use the relative path. One note for the record, regarding the network share: This will also make the R library directory read-only, so users cannot install packages, there. On the other hand, users can simply add a local library using Settings->Configure RKWard->R packages->R Library locations.

@kapatp: On windows, KDE (and rkward) stores its settings in the user\'s \"Application Data\" folder. the \".rkward\"-directory is placed in the user\'s \"Documents and Settings\"-folder by default. Thus, both should be read-write even in this setup. (As a side note: This also means that if there are several installations of KDE or RKWard on the machine, they will share one set of configuration flles).

The following forum message was posted by fsando at http://sourceforge.net/projects/rkward/forums/forum/165573/topic/1822409:

@6: I\'m sure your problem is specific to your setup. Or perhaps you\'re running a very old version of RKWard? I\'ve been running rkward for several years and I find it to be just as stable and reliable as any other (commercial or not) statistical program. Actually some commercial apps I work with are of remarkably low quality compared to rkward.

I can fully understand the frustration when things crash left and right, especially if the app looks as \"sexy\" as rkward and you really want it to work. But I would ask you to always treat the developers with respect. They are working hard and doing an awesome job from which the rest of us benefit so much.

The following forum message was posted by harrelfe at http://sourceforge.net/projects/rkward/forums/forum/165574/topic/3716805:

Here\'s an example:

if(1==2)
{
    y <- 1
}

is what appears.  I would like the { indented as per a popular R formatting style:

if(1==2)
    {
        y <- 1
    }

Having function arguments automatically indented in continuation lines would also be nice:

lm(y ~ x,
     data=d)

Frank

